 to the firm you're applying for  but it works both ways  after skimming through the job postings  you might find that right off the bat  this job description doesn't really  align with your interest or your skill  sets so just skip right through those  and don't waste too much time on them  so first of all you need to spend a  little bit of time thinking about your  unique strengths and whether it's  applicable to this job posting  and  and the work they do and if you do find  some overlap then you can start  strategizing on how you want to sell  your skills i'm going to give you an  example with my own application at the  firm that i'm at currently but before  that a little bit about my background so  before this firm i've mostly worked on  small local projects like houses or  small commercial or office renaults and  i really wanted to work on bigger public  projects and i really wanted to work at  like an internationally recognized firm  where you see the work and you're like  okay that's really high quality work but  as an architect you get hired based on  your previous experience so when the  majority of your experience is one type  of work  it's good in one way because you build a  lot of expertise in that one type but  it's really hard to get your foot in the  door with other building types  so i really had to sit down and  strategize on how i could sell my  experience as an asset so i've tailored  this entire application to the firm that  i'm at now which is faulkner browns  architect  which is like a mid-sized international  firm  with its main office in the uk  but it also has a small firm  um in vancouver  in a sense that the partners started a  satellite office here in vancouver um  two or three years ago  so  while it has a reputation and the  projects of a large firm  it still has the resources of a small  startup firm  so  i'm thinking  they're  probably looking for people who can kind  of get down and dirty and  really help the firm like the local  office grow i'm really trying to sell  the story of this person who's not just  a great designer but someone who's like  practical and flexible and who can do a  variety of things  and someone who can contribute to the  firm culture and really help them build  a local reputation like i said i've  worked in small local firms for four  years before this which could be seen as  a downside because i haven't worked on  any big international project but i  really wanted to spend that as my asset  so in my cover letter i said having  focused my professional growth in a  small young firm over the last three  years i have become a nimble team player  and a proactive learner and can quickly  develop skills in any area that requires  assistance  i also mentioned some extra skills i've  picked up i've participated in marketing  and promotions  including proposal writing and  coordination  website design social media which is in  a small firm things that the partner or  the principal usually has to do  and so you're showing them that yeah you  can take away time from  from the principal's workload i also  talk about my presentation skills  in kind of direct relation to my youtube  channel which has been a great great  great asset for me professionally  because a lot of firms want to see  that  you're doing things on the side that  contribute to the field of architecture  and  it shows your passion it shows that you  have all of these soft skills that  that do come in really handy when you're  an architect but are not necessarily  taught in architecture school it also  shows that you can teach yourself skills  right  um and then i also talked a little bit  about business management  again talking about like working in a  small young firm i've learned the ins  and outs of managing an architectural  business um during my recent sabbatical  i worked as a freelance designer  recent sabbatical is when um  i i left my job  just before the pandemic started so i  had a window of time where i couldn't  find a job during that time i worked  as a freelance designer  where i was working on small-scale  projects for different small clients and  this experience helped me learn how to  negotiate fees and give proposals and  and i think just most importantly making  sure that the client is satisfied  because i know that these are all things  that  a small  new office  is gonna find really valuable from an  additional member  and then in my resume  i try to  reinforce this idea of this nimble  person  with like hands-on experience  and in the job description  under work experience section i  um i talk about how i work closely with  the firm principles on all aspects of  architectural projects  plus  these are things that typically an  intern  wouldn't really be asked to do in a big  firm  but it could be very valuable in a young  growing firm and then when we get into  my portfolio i try to reinforce this  idea again by putting in a project that  i did from start to finish  at the very beginning of my portfolio  and you know this is a tiny  project in the world of architecture it  was like 5 000 square feet of renovation  and you know i actually  have projects that i've worked on  that are bigger that are larger scale  um but none of them i've actually done  from start to finish  if i was applying for a large firm  like a stantec or oma or something like  that i would probably try to focus on  completely different strengths like  modeling skills or detailing skills  um so yeah i think you really need to  assess the firm  and  do some strategic thinking when you're  uh making your application if you have  any questions up to this point just let  me know in the comments and i'll try to  get to them as fast as possible  just a few words on the layout and the  format before we get started  just use a standard size like a letter  or legal or tabloid  and if you're applying for a design  position  you should just use indesign it does  look  different from traditional word  and you know it's the first impression  of your work right so  you want to make it inviting you don't  want to make it too cluttered and  you want to make sure that they know  that you know how to use these tools and  give it just clear section headings like  lots of white space  and  yeah just make it easy to read use like  a 11  point font size  probably like 10.5 minimum the format  the most common way to present your  resume is probably the  like reverse chronological  um where you put your most recent  experience first but i've also seen  resumes where like if you don't have too  much relevant work experience or if  you're like looking for a career change  uh you can also  format it in a way that like focuses on  your skill sets today we're just going  to be covering the reverse chronological  format  a typical resume is going to have your  contact info your professional summary  your work experience education and your  skills  and  any other  achievements or awards or activities  professional summary personally  i don't really think it's necessary if  you're gonna put in a cover letter  because the cover letter is gonna  describe a little bit of your background  what you're interested in why you want  to work for the firm and in most cases  people  know why you're applying for the  position  so  yeah i don't know if you don't have  enough space i would say you can cut  that out  um yep and so you cover your  personal details contact info email  phone number address yeah i don't really  think it's necessary nobody's gonna be  mailing you stuff  so yeah i think it's you can leave that  out so summary your objective  this is kind of a two to three sentence  summary of your career and you can place  this at the very top of your resume  and yeah this is just going to give your  employer a very very quick impression of  who you are and what you're looking for  but like i said before if you're  covering this in the cover letter then  this might not be necessary  so in my case  i i put in the cover letter just before  my resume  um in a pdf but i've also done it where  i just send the cover letter in the body  of the email right  so  typically if you know  the name of the principal that you're  emailing and you know that the email is  going directly to someone who's gonna be  looking at your application like the  principal or the partner  then it makes sense to include the cover  letter in the body of the email  but if you know that your application's  gonna be passed around and you don't  know  the name of the person you're sending  the application to  then i would include the cover letter as  a part of the resume  yeah so what i was saying before is that  if you are including a cover letter as a  part of your resume no need to put in  the work summary i don't think it's  necessary but if you are including this  what you can include here is your job  title and your years of experience so  for example  licensed architect aibc with four years  of local experience  you can also  list out like one or two of your  achievements so you can say something  like completed a major renovation in the  city of coquitlam  on time and on budget  and  your desired goal so if you're looking  to  work more in the public sector then you  can say  looking to grow as an architect work on  larger public projects and then you  cover your education where you went to  school and when you went to school  please  please please write the dates of when  you went to school this is very  important before i had a separate  section where i listed all the awards  that i got but i decided to put it all  in this one section so that i could kind  of reduce the amount of text  i put my gpa in here as well  this is probably more important for like  fresh grads but maybe not  once you get a little bit further into  your career and i also listed some other  facts like um being one of three  students who graduate on the accelerated  program and like being on the dean's  list and stuff like that and then you  list your work experience and this is  obviously the most important section of  your resume  you should talk about where you worked  um when you worked there and what your  role was and you can just give a very  brief summary of the highlights of that  role and as well as some of the the  projects that you worked on  and then the portfolio is going to get  into this a little bit more in detail  it's going to talk about your projects  more in detail it's going to show your  design abilities it's going to show your  graphical abilities it's just going to  reinforce  your position as that role a little bit  more  um  yeah and make sure you list everything  in reverse chronological order with your  most recent and the most relevant  experience first  and you know if there's some gaps in  your history  you should be prepared to explain that  most cases they will  ask about that i had a question  from one of my subscribers that was  asking  how you should talk about  lengthy  gap in your employment history and i  actually answered it in one of my q a  sessions so i'll just put the link to  that over here um and then yeah under  achievements you can list out any awards  like i said i decided to bump it into my  education just because a lot of the  awards that i got were from school and  then you can also mention your top soft  and hard skills  so  i kind of separated out the software  just because it's such an important part  of an architecture job you know for a  young architect like knowing certain  software is kind of the difference  between getting hired and not so yeah  like make sure that you know the  software that the firm uses  and usually they'll describe that in the  job description  and under skills i listed out my  architectural skills as well as some of  my non-architectural meta skills you can  also include  languages and hobbies i find languages  to be pretty  helpful especially if you can speak a  language 